{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And if You deal thus with me, etc.</b> That is, he feared that HaShem would say to him to bear it against his will, even though he would not be able, on this condition let him serve HaShem to the point of the soul's departure, as it is written (Deuteronomy 6:5): ״בְּכָל נַפְשְׁךָ״. Therefore he said <b>and if</b>, etc., <b>kill me now</b> — meaning, now he delivered his soul and would no longer bear the burden alone. And he gave the reason: <b>and let me not see my wretchedness</b> — meaning, a thing that causes harm to the soul, about which the righteous are scrupulous. And the evil over which his heart cried out is that if they kill him, Israel would incur annihilation, and it would turn out that he caused them to be punished. This too is reckoned as affliction of the soul, after the statement (Proverbs 17:26): ״גַּם עֲנוֹשׁ לַצַּדִּיק״, etc." "And it repeated to say <b>kill me now, I pray You, kill</b>, to say that he is already slain if HaShem does not kill him, for surely Israel would kill him; and if so, when HaShem kills him He is killing none other than an already-slain man — גַּבְרָא קְטִילָא קָטִיל, and the word ״הָרוֹג״ is read with the shuruk vowel. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Yalkut Numbers 735; Midrash Tehillim 23), and this is their language: if I do not give [in to] them, they kill me, and if I speak against You, You kill me. And the verse intends all of these ways."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:41.746745256Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}